1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,190
>> Right now, you're moving around the Sun at about 30 km/s.

2
00:00:06,270 --> 00:00:16,080
>> But not steadily. In January, Earth moves about 1 km/s faster than in July.

3
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:23,120
>> The rule behind that was found more than 400 years ago, in old records of the planet Mars.

4
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:29,540
And it started with an error of eight arcminutes: about a quarter of the width of the Moon.

5
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:33,510
>> A quarter of the Moon? That's all?

6
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:41,110
>> That's all. This is the story of Kepler's second law: equal areas in equal times.

7
00:00:45,910 --> 00:00:53,230
In the late 1500s, the best measurements of the sky came from a Danish nobleman, Tycho Brahe.

8
00:00:53,310 --> 00:01:02,300
>> From 1576 he built Uraniborg, on the island of Hven, with the king's money.

9
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:08,120
>> No telescopes yet: Tycho measured by eye, with large quadrants and sextants.

10
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:15,070
>> His positions came close to one arcminute: a sixtieth of a degree.

11
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:21,910
For scale, the full Moon is about 31 arcminutes across.

12
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:28,510
>> Year after year, he recorded where the planets were. Mars was among them.

13
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:38,200
>> In 1597 he left Denmark. Soon he was working for the emperor, in Prague.

14
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:45,550
>> And in February 1600, a 28-year-old mathematician joined him: Johannes Kepler.

15
00:01:45,630 --> 00:01:52,070
>> Tycho guarded his data, and gave Kepler one planet: Mars.

16
00:01:52,150 --> 00:02:01,940
>> Then in October 1601, Tycho died after a short illness. Two days later, Kepler took over as imperial mathematician.

17
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:11,120
Tycho's heirs owned the observations, which would delay Kepler's book for years. But Kepler had the Mars data.

18
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:22,660
Now, astronomers had long modelled the planets with circles. Even Copernicus, who put the Sun near the middle, used circles.

19
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:27,030
>> Circles riding on circles, in his case.

20
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:34,350
>> So Kepler began with a circle too, with the Sun off-centre, and a point from which the motion looks steady.

21
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:37,470
>> That point is called the equant.

22
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:43,640
>> Fitting all that to Tycho's numbers meant one long calculation, over and over again.

23
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:50,530
He complained in print: "Take pity on me, who had to go through with at least seventy repetitions of it."

24
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:53,580
>> Seventy times, by hand.

25
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:59,650
And it nearly worked: within two arcminutes of Tycho's positions.

26
00:02:59,730 --> 00:03:04,720
>> But at other points in the orbit, it missed by up to eight.

27
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,990
>> Couldn't he call eight close enough?

28
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:13,960
>> Not with Tycho's data. It was far too accurate for an error that size to be ignored.

29
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:22,280
Kepler wrote: "These eight minutes alone will lead us along a path to the reform of the whole of Astronomy."

30
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:26,020
>> Quite a promise, for eight arcminutes.

31
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:33,220
>> Something in his model was wrong: the circle, or the way the planet moved along it.

32
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:43,810
Kepler wanted a physical cause. He imagined a force from the Sun, pushing the planets round, weaker with distance.

33
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:52,230
>> So a planet's speed would fall as its distance grows: twice as far, half as fast.

34
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:58,750
>> But to find where the planet would be, he'd have to add up its distance from the Sun all along the way.

35
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:02,820
>> Every point of the orbit? By hand?

36
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:10,920
>> Exactly. So by late 1602 he'd found a shortcut: measure the area swept by the line from the Sun.

37
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,590
Picture each day's motion as a thin triangle: its height is the distance from the Sun, its base the distance travelled.

38
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:25,210
>> Tall and narrow far away, short and wide close in.

39
00:04:25,290 --> 00:04:32,480
>> And if speed falls as distance grows, every day's triangle has the same area.

40
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:39,040
Equal areas in equal times. And oddly, Kepler found this second law before the first.

41
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:43,310
>> So the numbers aren't the order he found them in.

42
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:52,230
>> No. And his speed rule only works for orbits that are nearly circles. The area rule works for every orbit.

43
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:59,520
Now he needed the shape. He tried egg-shaped ovals, again and again.

44
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,160
>> How many?

45
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:07,700
>> About forty failed attempts. Then, in late 1604, he tried an ellipse.

46
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:13,740
>> He'd thought it too simple for earlier astronomers to have missed.

47
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:21,860
>> But it fitted. He'd even had the formula earlier, without seeing it. He wrote: "Ah, what a foolish bird I have been!"

48
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:29,710
An ellipse has two special points, its foci. The Sun sits at one. The other is empty.

49
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:36,660
>> That's Kepler's first law: an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

50
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:43,010
>> Mars's ellipse is barely squashed: it's less than half a percent narrower than it is long.

51
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:49,110
But the Sun is well off-centre: more than 9% of the way from the middle to the end.

52
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:56,830
>> Only Mercury's orbit is more stretched. Lucky that his planet was Mars.

53
00:05:56,910 --> 00:06:04,480
>> In 1609 he published it all in a book: Astronomia nova, the New Astronomy.

54
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:13,040
So where is a planet on a given day? Kepler turned the area rule into an equation.

55
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:20,910
>> Time gives an angle, M, that ticks round steadily, like a clock hand.

56
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:28,860
>> The planet's real place is set by another angle, E. And the little e says how stretched the ellipse is.

57
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:36,480
Put together, that's Kepler's equation: M equals E, minus e times the sine of E.

58
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:39,930
>> And you know M, but you want E.

59
00:06:40,010 --> 00:06:48,150
>> No algebra can solve it for E. So in 1621, Kepler suggested guessing, then improving the guess.

60
00:06:48,230 --> 00:06:57,170
For Mars, start with E equal to M, put each answer back in, and within four rounds the digits stop changing.

61
00:06:57,250 --> 00:07:01,990
>> So it's never exact, just ever closer.

62
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:09,410
>> Right. This animation does that for every frame. Here's a stretched orbit, so the effect is easy to see.

63
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:19,330
>> Split one trip around into twelve equal times, and shade the area each one sweeps.

64
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:25,840
>> Near the Sun, the wedges are short and fat. Far away, they're long and thin.

65
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:28,260
>> They look nothing alike.

66
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:34,960
>> But measure them, and each one is a twelfth of the ellipse: 8.3%.

67
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:40,330
>> While the distance travelled in each is wildly different.

68
00:07:40,410 --> 00:07:49,710
>> On this orbit, the planet moves four times faster at its closest point, perihelion, than at aphelion, its farthest.

69
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:58,780
>> Earth's orbit is much rounder. Does the rule still hold?

70
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:01,200
>> It does, just more gently.

71
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:09,740
Earth passes perihelion around 3 January, 147.1 million km from the Sun

72
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:18,690
>> and aphelion around 4 July, at 152.1 million km.

73
00:08:18,770 --> 00:08:29,110
>> So it's fastest in January, about 30.3 km/s, and slowest in July, about 29.3.

74
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:39,210
>> But it shows on the calendar: March equinox to September takes about 186 days.

75
00:08:39,290 --> 00:08:48,060
>> And the other half of the year takes only about 179, because Earth passes perihelion then, moving faster.

76
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:54,980
So in the north, spring and summer together last about a week longer than autumn and winter.

77
00:08:55,060 --> 00:09:05,680
>> Mars swings more: 26.5 km/s near the Sun, 22 at the far end.

78
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:15,070
>> Comets show it best. Halley's Comet dips to about 0.6 AU from the Sun, then out to about 35.

79
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:22,040
>> One AU being Earth's average distance from the Sun.

80
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:24,360
>> So out beyond Neptune.

81
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:34,810
>> Where, in December 2023, it was crawling along at 0.91 km/s.

82
00:09:34,890 --> 00:09:44,980
>> At the two ends, speed times distance is the same. So near the Sun, it races along at about 54 km/s.

83
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:50,580
>> Fifty-nine times closer, fifty-nine times faster.

84
00:09:50,660 --> 00:10:01,810
>> Of its roughly 75-year orbit, it spends only about two years closer to the Sun than Jupiter. It's due back in 2061.

85
00:10:05,010 --> 00:10:10,780
Kepler wasn't done. He wanted a rule that linked all the planets, not just one.

86
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:18,400
>> By his own account, it came to him on 8 March 1618.

87
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:26,220
>> Square a planet's time to go round. Cube its distance from the Sun. The ratio is the same for every planet.

88
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:28,490
>> Try it with Jupiter.

89
00:10:28,570 --> 00:10:36,010
>> Jupiter takes 11.86 years to go round. Squared, that's about 141.

90
00:10:36,090 --> 00:10:45,960
>> And it's 5.2 AU out. Cubed: about 141 again.

91
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:51,980
>> With Kepler's own numbers, all six known planets agree to within about two percent.

92
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:56,400
>> "I first believed I was dreaming," he wrote.

93
00:10:56,480 --> 00:11:00,170
>> After forty failed ovals, you can see why.

94
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:07,840
>> It's his third law, in his book of 1619, The Harmony of the World.

95
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:15,760
>> There, Kepler heard music in the orbits. Earth's turning speed, seen from the Sun, varies by about a semitone.

96
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,560
>> The step from mi to fa.

97
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:24,950
>> "The Earth sings Mi, Fa, Mi," he wrote: you may hear "misery" and "famine" in the syllables.

98
00:11:25,030 --> 00:11:30,520
In 1621, his Epitome gave the area law its modern form.

99
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:38,720
>> He also noted that the third law fits Jupiter's four bright moons.

100
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:45,480
So why should planets obey these rules at all?

101
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:48,220
>> That's where Isaac Newton comes in.

102
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:56,290
In 1684 he proved that anything pulled toward a fixed centre sweeps out equal areas in equal times.

103
00:11:56,370 --> 00:11:59,340
>> Anything? Not just planets?

104
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:08,530
>> Anything. His proof, in the Principia of 1687, is beautifully simple. Imagine the pull comes in short, sharp tugs.

105
00:12:08,610 --> 00:12:14,320
>> With no pull, the planet goes straight, in equal steps.

106
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:20,790
>> Each step makes a triangle with the Sun. Same base, same height: same area.

107
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:24,940
>> Equal steps, along one straight line.

108
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:31,160
>> Now a tug toward the Sun bends the path. But it pulls only along the line to the Sun.

109
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:39,030
>> So the new triangle keeps its base and height. Same area again.

110
00:12:39,110 --> 00:12:46,100
>> They share the base from the Sun to B, and the tug slides c parallel to it, so the height stays put.

111
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:55,370
>> Make the tugs small and frequent, and you get a smooth orbit, with equal areas.

112
00:12:56,070 --> 00:13:02,560
>> Notice what the proof never needed: how strong the pull is. Any pull toward a centre will do.

113
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:07,210
>> Even one that grows with distance, like this?

114
00:13:07,290 --> 00:13:12,360
>> Even that. Today we call this conservation of angular momentum.

115
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:21,010
The ellipse and the third law need more: a pull that weakens with the square of the distance, as gravity does.

116
00:13:24,210 --> 00:13:33,050
Funny thing: Kepler never called these laws. The word first appeared in print in 1738, in a book by Voltaire.

117
00:13:33,130 --> 00:13:38,020
>> And the tidy set of three came in 1804.

118
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:43,210
>> So, one: a planet's orbit is an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

119
00:13:43,290 --> 00:13:47,280
>> Two: equal areas in equal times.

120
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:52,150
>> Three: the period squared goes with the distance cubed.

121
00:13:52,230 --> 00:13:55,990
All because Kepler refused to ignore eight arcminutes.

122
00:13:56,070 --> 00:13:59,080
>> A quarter of the Moon's width.

123
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:08,300
>> So this January, when it's coldest in the north, remember: Earth is at its closest to the Sun, and moving fastest.
